{
  "title": "CHAPTER 16 — INDIAN NATIONAL MOVEMENT (1857–1947)",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "t5b5yak",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. BACKGROUND OF NATIONALISM"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Indian National Congress (INC) was founded in 1885 by A.O. Hume, with its first session in Bombay presided over by W.C. Bonnerjee and attended by 72 delegates.",
        "The Ilbert Bill (1883) proposed by Lord Ripon sought to allow Indian judges to try Europeans, exposing colonial racial discrimination when met with strong opposition.",
        "The Vernacular Press Act of 1878 was passed by Lord Lytton to suppress Indian language newspapers and was later repealed by Lord Ripon in 1882.",
        "C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru formed the Swaraj Party in 1923 to enter legislative councils and obstruct colonial governance.",
        "The Individual Satyagraha launched in 1940 featured Vinoba Bhave as the first Satyagrahi and Jawaharlal Nehru as the second."
      ],
      "id": "8n8hag7"
    },
    {
